            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The discriminant ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\Delta=(a-4)^2",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " is the square of a rational number, so the quadratic formula gives rational roots.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2x^2+ax+(a-2)&=0\\\\\\Delta&=a^2-4(2)(a-2)\\\\\\Delta&=a^2-8a+16\\\\\\Delta&=(a-4)^2\\end{align*}$$\nSince $a$ is rational, $\\sqrt{\\Delta}=|a-4|$ is rational. The quadratic formula gives\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-a\\pm\\sqrt{\\Delta}}{4}\\\\x&=\\frac{-a\\pm|a-4|}{4}.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth numerators are rational and the denominator is nonzero, so both solutions are rational. The solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=1-\\frac{a}{2}$; they coincide when $a=4$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
